   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.8",
    "topic_title": "The Tangent Function",
    "stem": "What is the least positive period of f(θ) = 2tan(3θ) + 1?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "π/3",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Tangent has base period π. The coefficient of θ inside tangent is b = 3, so the period is π/|3| = π/3; the outside multiplier and vertical shift do not change it."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "2π/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sine-and-cosine period confusion: this uses 2π as tangent’s base period. Tangent’s base period is π, so dividing 2π by 3 gives twice the least positive period. Fix: Identify the trig function’s base period before dividing by |b|."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "3π",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Horizontal-scaling reversal: this multiplies the base period by 3 instead of dividing. Multiplying θ by 3 makes tangent complete each cycle over a shorter input interval. Fix: Divide π by the absolute value of the coefficient of θ."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "π/6",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Vertical-factor confusion: this treats the outside multiplier 2 as another horizontal multiplier. Only the inside coefficient 3 affects the period; the outside 2 scales the outputs. Fix: Locate the coefficient inside tangent’s argument and use only that coefficient in π/|b|."
     }
    ]
   },
